\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Hidden-state trajectories for synthetic inputs at Layer~8.} Each row shows a different input waveform (left) and its 2D PCA trajectory through PT, FT, and RI (colored by phase). Percentages show variance captured by 2 PCs. PT produces thin, low-variance trajectories (it was not trained on these tokens). FT creates structured, high-variance loops---especially for periodic inputs (sine: 51\%, square: 95\%). RI produces geometrically distinct solutions: clean circles for periodic inputs but using more total dimensions. The two-frequency input reveals the starkest contrast: FT produces a compact trajectory while RI forms a complex multi-loop orbit.}}{7}{figure.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:synthetic}{{5}{7}{\textbf {Hidden-state trajectories for synthetic inputs at Layer~8.} Each row shows a different input waveform (left) and its 2D PCA trajectory through PT, FT, and RI (colored by phase). Percentages show variance captured by 2 PCs. PT produces thin, low-variance trajectories (it was not trained on these tokens). FT creates structured, high-variance loops---especially for periodic inputs (sine: 51\%, square: 95\%). RI produces geometrically distinct solutions: clean circles for periodic inputs but using more total dimensions. The two-frequency input reveals the starkest contrast: FT produces a compact trajectory while RI forms a complex multi-loop orbit}{figure.caption.29}{}}
